edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186539964 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "736c5de7-2fbd-5a65-8262-e8bce4536075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540014 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540014 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540014 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540014 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540014 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540014 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540014 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540014 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540014 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f24e3ec2-08cb-5869-8ee2-41217f338f4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540021 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186539877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540021 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540021 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540021 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186539877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540021 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe7bf0ba-27c6-5e85-90fc-d44843ac230f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159642" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540024 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=30$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=32$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=12.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=22$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12431c38-5ac6-59aa-ba15-2add6471e563" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540024 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=22-16=6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{30}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{32}}≈6.380\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 6\\pm 6.380\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540042 #edb/ref 1071 "It is known that the thickness of ceramic tiles from suppliers X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=1.6\\,\\text{mm}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=1.2\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively. A construction contractor wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the thickness of ceramic tiles from supplier X is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the thickness of ceramic tiles from supplier Y. He constructs a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the contractor sampled $20$ tiles from supplier X and $18$ tiles from supplier Y and found that their mean thicknesses are $18.8\\,\\text{mm}$ and $18.5\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $0.3\\pm 0.894.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34a9b0ce-4c8f-5634-aaa8-208faeda7581" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540042 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=18.8-18.5=0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{1.6}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{18}}≈0.894\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.3\\pm 0.894\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $0.3\\pm 0.894.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$\n\n$$\n(0.3-0.894,\\,0.3+0.894)=(-0.594,\\,1.194)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540051 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540051 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159626" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540051 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540051 #edb/ref 1071 "The weights of the wooden and steel chairs produced by a furniture company have standard deviations of $2\\,\\text{lb}$ and $1\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively. The company wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of weights for the wooden chairs is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of weights for the steel chairs. They construct a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the company sampled $100$ wooden chairs and $150$ steel chairs and found that their mean weights are $10\\,\\text{lb}$ and $9\\,\\text{lb},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $1\\pm 0.423.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540051 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5391d4bc-fe22-5a3b-85bc-6502aec7782e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540051 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that \n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=10-9=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.960:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.960\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{100}+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{150}}≈0.423\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 0.423\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1\\pm 0.423.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(1-0.423,\\,1+0.423)=(0.577,\\,1.423)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540099 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540099 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540099 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540099 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=32$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=28$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=19$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=11,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db9cf9a1-6584-5405-8a36-efa72137f427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540099 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=19-11=8\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{32}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{28}}≈3.118\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 8\\pm 3.118\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186539932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540111 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540111 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540111 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=58$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=86$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=34$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=43,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540111 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9f98bfe-eda2-5f27-b0c5-b96f1c4792b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540111 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=34-43=-9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3.4}^{2}}{58}+\\frac{{4.9}^{2}}{86}}≈1.356\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -9\\pm 1.356\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540137 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186540170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540137 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4d34c056-1894-58b9-beb3-f82d4263465e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186540137 #edb/ref 1063 #edb